<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Guard (Morocco)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abid al-Bukhari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Guard, or Abid al-Bukhari, was an army of enslaved black soldiers created by the Moroccan sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulay-ismail">Moulay Ismail</a> from the 1670s. Its men were drawn from slaves and from black Moroccans forcibly enlisted, and they swore loyalty on a copy of al-Bukhari's collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>. It gave the sultan power independent of the tribes, but after his death in 1727 it made and unmade sultans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-guard-morocco.jpg" alt="Black Guard (Morocco)" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugène Delacroix · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eug%C3%A8ne_Ferdinand_Victor_Delacroix_044.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
